# verus/seams.rules: the port's rules for the kit's seam ledger generator
# (kit/verus/seams; its doc comment is the full format). One rule a line,
# fields separated by one tab, `#` comments. The generator scans SOURCE
# for the items Verus takes on trust and writes one ledger file per
# source file with seams under LEDGER; these lines are what is the
# port's: where things are, the tools and categories its SEAMS.md names,
# and the heuristics that propose a category for a seam no overrides line
# names. This starting point is challoc's (verus/seams.sh as of its
# deda0db); reshape the lists and the guesses to the port's code.
#
# Where the ledger and the sources are, and the command that writes the
# ledger (named in each ledger file's header). The theseus-proved-port
# package installs the generator as proved-port-seams (on PATH; a
# checkout's kit/install.sh installs it too); a port that runs it some
# other way writes here how.
ledger	verus/ledger
source	src
command	proved-port-seams -write
seams-doc	verus/SEAMS.md
#
# Test files under SOURCE: not scanned for seams, read only for `unsafe
# impl Send/Sync` (a claims nothing status refuses a type that has one).
# A pattern with a / matches the path from the root, any other the base
# name; * and ? match / too, as in find -path.
test-file	tests.rs
test-file	testutil.rs
test-file	src/*/tests/*
#
# The tools, in the order the totals tables list them, and the one for
# a seam no check applies to.
tool	Islaris/Isla
tool	RefinedRust
tool	Kani
tool	loom/GenMC
tool	Verus
tool	ABI tests
tool	none (assumption)
assumption-tool	none (assumption)
#
# The categories, in table order, each with the tool proposed for it.
category	inline asm	Islaris/Isla
category	FFI/syscall	none (assumption)
category	function pointer	Kani
category	UnsafeCell/volatile/transmute	RefinedRust
category	raw pointer (internal)	RefinedRust
category	raw pointer from C caller	none (assumption)
category	panic/abort	Kani
category	atomics primitive	loom/GenMC
category	platform/kernel fact	none (assumption)
category	C-program contract	none (assumption)
category	termination	loom/GenMC
category	core library	Kani
category	allocator invariant	RefinedRust
category	C ABI export	ABI tests
#
# A status holding `TAG: ` cites a check (a claims nothing status may
# not); -check fails on a discharged or partly checked status that holds
# none, and on a `TAG: ` that names nothing. A third field `partial`
# marks a check that covers only part of a contract (challoc's loom
# models run a copy of the code, and its GenMC cases erase the
# permissions): -check fails a discharged status that cites one.
cite-tag	Kani
cite-tag	const assert
cite-tag	loom	partial
cite-tag	GenMC	partial
#
# -check resolves the citations of the tags these lines name and fails
# each that does not resolve. A cite-fn tag cites `a::b::m::f`: a fn
# with the attribute line `#[ATTR]` above it, in SOURCE/a/b.rs (or
# SOURCE/a/b/mod.rs) and its inline `mod m`, laid out as rustfmt lays it
# out, with no cfg, cfg_attr or ignore attribute on the fn but
# `#[cfg(CFG)]` for a CFG field after ATTR (the cfg the tool builds
# under: `kani` here, `test` for a #[test] fn). A cite-module NAME reads a path starting `NAME::` under DIR (here
# challoc's `kani_proofs::m::f`, f in src/kani/m.rs). A cite-assert tag
# cites `LABEL in FILE`, FILE a .rs file under SOURCE or a cite-module
# DIR and not a test file: one top-level `const _: () = assert!(` right
# under a `// LABEL` comment block, with no attribute above it. A
# cite-row tag's citations each also need a row in FILE's table under
# `## HEADING` that names the citation in backquotes in its first cell
# and holds every PART. A cite-test tag cites `MOD::TEST`: a top-level
# #[test] fn TEST in DIR/MOD.rs, not #[should_panic] (a control), under
# no cfg, cfg_attr or ignore attribute but `#[cfg(CFG)]` for a CFG field,
# whose doc comment or body names the seam's function in backquotes, in
# a file that names the seam's source file in backquotes and that
# DIR/lib.rs declares with `mod MOD;`, DIR/../Cargo.toml keeping
# DIR/lib.rs the library root with its tests on. A cite-case tag cites
# `CASE [MODEL ...]`: the one line of DIR/cases (`name | template |
# models | constants | expected`) naming CASE, expecting `pass` alone,
# run under exactly those models in that order, whose DIR/TEMPLATE.rs
# names the seam's source file and function in backquotes. Any other
# tag is the port's own check to resolve, from seams -cites (one line
# per citation).
cite-fn	Kani	kani::proof	kani
cite-module	kani_proofs	src/kani
cite-assert	const assert
# challoc's loom models, `#[cfg(test)] mod m;` lines in tests/loom/src/lib.rs:
# cite-test	loom	tests/loom/src	test
# challoc's GenMC cases, tests/genmc/cases as tests/genmc/run.sh reads it:
# cite-case	GenMC	tests/genmc
# challoc's guard tests: `callers: PATH` names a #[test] fn with its row
# in the guard-test table:
# cite-tag	callers
# cite-fn	callers	test	test
# cite-row	callers	verus/README.md	Guard tests	Mutants killed:	Known escapes:
#
# challoc's not-Copy checks: each `tracked` struct or enum needs a check
# in the file that it is neither Copy nor Clone (`<T as NotCopy<_>>::HOLDS`
# and `<T as NotClone<_>>::HOLDS` under a `// `T`` label), and a
# tracked-type seam citing `const assert:` cites its type's check. Write
# a generic type's check at Copy arguments (`K<u8>`): a const assertion
# checks only the arguments it names, so one at a non-Copy argument
# passes for a type that is Copy whenever its argument is:
# notcopy	src/vspec/notcopy.rs	const assert
#
# challoc's Kani pins: a harness that runs a copy of a body (Kani has no
# model of inline assembly) pins the original's lines and the copy's in
# the file, `SRC<TAB>LINE[<TAB>NAME:ROLE]` rows, and -check holds them in
# place (and the copy equal to the original but for its `-only` rows):
# pins	verus/kani/pins.txt
#
# The vulnerability-class catalog, once it is filled in from
# verus/VULN-CLASSES.md's template and keeps its layout: -check fails a
# theorem cited `ID (#n)` with no verus/theorems/ID.md, a row's class
# with no `###` section, and a `gap: #n` that is not an item of Gaps (or
# an item no row or section cites), a ruled out by cell that cites
# neither form and is neither `not shipped` nor `by construction:`, and
# a class section whose **Proved.** or **Gap.** paragraph is missing or
# cites neither form (a paragraph with nothing to cite says "none"). A
# catalog in another layout leaves the line out:
# vuln-classes	verus/VULN-CLASSES.md
#
# The heuristics: the first guess whose conditions all hold proposes its
# category (- for none, which -check reports until an overrides line
# names one). FIELD=RE matches, FIELD!RE does not; kind, name, file and
# body (comments and literals blanked) are the fields, RE is RE2.
guess	termination	kind=^no_decreases$
guess	C ABI export	kind=^unsupported-attribute$
guess	inline asm	body=asm!
guess	atomics primitive	kind=^(assume_spec|ext_type_spec|ext_trait_spec|spec_impl)$	name=Atomic
guess	UnsafeCell/volatile/transmute	kind=^(assume_spec|ext_type_spec|ext_trait_spec|spec_impl)$	name=UnsafeCell|volatile|transmute
guess	raw pointer (internal)	kind=^(assume_spec|ext_type_spec|ext_trait_spec|spec_impl)$	name=^(core::)?ptr::|^<\*|::add$|::offset$|from_raw_parts|null
guess	-	kind=^(assume_spec|ext_type_spec|ext_trait_spec|spec_impl)$	name=^crate::|^super::
guess	core library	kind=^(assume_spec|ext_type_spec|ext_trait_spec|spec_impl)$
# challoc's C entry points are under src/api/: a pointer there came
# from the C caller.
guess	raw pointer from C caller	kind=^(ghost_)?assume_new$	file=^src/api/
guess	raw pointer (internal)	kind=^(ghost_)?assume_new$
guess	FFI/syscall	body=(raw::)?syscall[0-6]?\(
guess	function pointer	body=\(\*?[a-z_.]*\.0\)\(|[^a-z]fn\(
guess	panic/abort	body=panic!	body!write!|writeln!
guess	function pointer	body=format_args!|write!|writeln!|panic!
guess	UnsafeCell/volatile/transmute	body=volatile|UnsafeCell|transmute|\.get\(\)
guess	atomics primitive	body=compare_exchange|fetch_[a-z]+\(|\.load\(|\.store\(
guess	raw pointer from C caller	body=\*mut|\*const|ptr::|as_ptr|\.add\(|from_raw_parts	file=^src/api/
guess	raw pointer (internal)	body=\*mut|\*const|ptr::|as_ptr|\.add\(|from_raw_parts
#
# The note on each unsupported-attribute entry (an item marked
# `#[verifier::assume_unsupported_attributes]`): what backs the symbol.
unsupported-doc	Verus takes every attribute it refuses on this item on trust (`#[verifier::assume_unsupported_attributes]`), its symbol and section attributes among them; the port's C ABI tests call the symbol.
#
# A port whose exports come from a macro names it, its file, the form of
# an invocation's `fn` lines and the `fn $VAR(` templates whose
# attributes each export takes (one for every name, or one per name).
# challoc's `verified_export_pair! { fn malloc / je_malloc(..) }` writes
# two, from `fn $name(` and `fn $je(` (the older `pair` line says the
# same: pair<TAB>MACRO<TAB>FILE<TAB>VAR<TAB>VAR):
# export-macro	verified_export_pair	src/api/mod.rs	slash	name	je
# A list form, `fn body [f_8, f_16, f_32] (..)` (the names may run over
# lines to the `]`), writes one export per name; invoked as
# `width::exports! {` too:
# export-macro	exports	src/width.rs	list	name
